            <ArticleTitle>Compliance among general practitioners in France with recommendations not to prescribe bed rest for acute low back pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>56-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">Acute low back pain is a very common reason for general practitioner visits.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate practices among French general practitioners regarding the prescription of bed rest in patients with low back pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">2000 general practitioners were to include the first three patients presenting within the first 3 d of onset of acute low back pain without nerve root pain. The characteristics of the pain, social and demographic characteristics of the patients, and treatments prescribed by the physicians were recorded.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">5355 patients were included, of whom more than half had a history of one or more episodes of low back pain. The mean pain severity score on a 100-mm visual analog scale was 57.2 +/- 22.7 mm. Bed rest was recommended to 27.6% of the patients, for a mean duration of 4.4 d. Patients to whom bed rest was recommended were significantly more likely than the other patients to have chronic low back pain (32.1% vs. 26.5%) and received a greater number of medications. Bed rest was associated with significantly poorer outcomes in the multivariable analysis adjusting for confounding factors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Recommendations to avoid bed rest in patients with acute low back pain are widely followed in France. Furthermore, our results confirm that bed rest is associated with poorer outcomes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
